            'faqs' => [
                ['Why are proactive and reactive monitoring taught separately?', '<p>Because they are different activities with different purposes. Proactive monitoring means looking for problems before anything goes wrong — inspections, checks, measuring whether controls are actually in place. Reactive monitoring means examining what happened after an incident or near miss and feeding that back into the system. Organisations that do only the second are permanently behind. Giving each its own subject makes that distinction impossible to miss.</p>', 1],
                ['Does this certify me as a safety officer?', '<p>It is a regulated Level 5 qualification in the subject, not an appointment or a licence. Who may act as a competent person, safety officer or adviser is determined by the law and the regulator where you work, and frequently by your employer\'s own requirements too. Qualifi publishes nothing about those positions. Confirm what your jurisdiction and employer actually require before relying on any qualification to satisfy it.</p>', 2],
                ['The page links an IIRSM leaflet. What does that mean?', '<p>Qualifi\'s page carries a link to a joint leaflet with the International Institute of Risk and Safety Management dated July 2023. We have not read that document, so we are not going to tell you what it establishes. What we will not do is turn a linked leaflet into a claim of membership, recognition or exemption. If that relationship matters to your plans, ask Qualifi or IIRSM directly.</p>', 3],
                ['Are there published learning outcomes?', '<p>Not on the qualification page. There is a link pointing to an outcomes section, but no outcomes appear in the page itself. Rather than fabricate them, the coverage shown here is taken from the topics Qualifi lists in its overview and from the subject titles, and it is labelled as such. The Centre Specification, updated in October 2025, is where the formal outcomes will be found.</p>', 4],
                ['Can I study while working full-time on site?', '<p>Qualifi says the qualification is suitable for part-time learners in the workplace, and adds that it works equally for full-time learners who take a formal placement or part-time employment. So it is designed with working people in mind. The practical answer, though, depends on how your approved centre delivers it — evenings, blocks, online or in person — and that is worth settling before you enrol.</p>', 5],
                ['What comes after it?', '<p>Three doors are listed on the official page: Qualifi\'s own Level 6 Diploma in Occupational Health and Safety Management, degree programmes in higher education, and going straight into work in an associated profession. Most of its Level 5 awards name only one or two, so three is generous by comparison. Qualifi also runs occupational health and safety qualifications at Level 7, so the ladder continues well above this point.</p>', 6],
            ],
            'meta_title' => 'Level 5 Diploma in Occupational Health & Safety | Qualifi',
