            'sort_order' => 252,
            'is_active' => false,
            'hero' => 'Sixty credits, four subjects, and a clear line drawn: process safety is about the plant, not the people walking past it. HAZOP and inherently safer design are named outright. Entry asks for an engineering or technical discipline.',
            'overview' => [
                'Two disciplines share a word and get confused constantly. Occupational safety is about people at work — slips, exposure, protective equipment. Process safety is about the plant: the reaction that runs away, the vessel that over-pressurises, the design decision taken years earlier that nobody revisited. This qualification is firmly the second kind, and the content makes that plain even though the page never spells the distinction out.',
                'Four subjects carry it. Process Safety Management Systems. Risk Based Process Safety. Process Safety Culture and Human Factors. Development as a Strategic Manager. Behind them sit eight published outcomes that name real technique: applying the principles of inherently safer design, analysing the anatomy of process safety incidents using HAZOP — hazard and operability study — and assessing what artificial intelligence and machine learning are changing in the field.',
                'Two things set it apart. It is 60 credits rather than 120, half the size of its sister safety awards. And its entry requirements are the most specialised anywhere here: an engineering degree, or a qualification in chemical, instrumentation, mechanical or a related discipline. Framework number 603/7608/9.',
            ],
