            ],
            'faqs' => [
                ['Are there five subjects or seven?', '<p>Probably seven, though we have left the published list untouched. Qualifi prints five bullets, but two of them clearly run two titles together: "Fundamentals of Predictive Modelling Advanced Predictive Modelling" and "Unsupervised Multivariate Methods Machine Learning". The published outcomes treat predictive modelling, data reduction and machine learning as separate areas, which supports reading them as four distinct subjects rather than two. Correcting an awarding body\'s own list is not our place, so we have flagged it instead. The October 2020 specification will give the definitive count.</p>', 1],
                ['Do I need to know how to code before starting?', '<p>Qualifi does not say, which is itself worth knowing. The published outcome is that you will develop enough skill in R, Python and SQL to carry out advanced analysis, phrasing that suggests the languages are taught rather than assumed. Entry is based on a Level 6 qualification, a degree or three years of relevant experience, with no coding test mentioned. In practice, arriving with some familiarity will make the statistical subjects far less punishing. Ask the centre what prior exposure its intake typically has.</p>', 2],
                ['Is a 2020 specification too old for this subject?', '<p>Judge it on what is in it rather than on the date alone. Statistical inference, predictive modelling, time series analysis and multivariate methods are stable foundations, and R, Python and SQL remain the working tools of most analytics teams. What has moved fastest since 2020 sits above that layer: large language models, newer frameworks, the way teams deploy models into production. Ask your centre directly when it last refreshed the taught material and what tooling its assignments use, because centres revise delivery independently of the specification.</p>', 3],
                ['Why does the page call it a postgraduate diploma?', '<p>Qualifi\'s own description uses that phrase, while the registered title on the framework is simply "Level 7 Diploma in Data Science". We use the registered title throughout, since that is what will appear on your certificate and what a university or employer will verify. The distinction is not pedantry: "postgraduate diploma" carries specific expectations in UK higher education, usually of a university award, and this is a regulated vocational qualification from an awarding body. Quote the registered title and the qualification number in any application.</p>', 4],
                ['What will a centre charge?', '<p>That is entirely the centre\'s decision. What Qualifi contributes is the design, the regulation and the final certificate; who teaches you, when, and for how much is settled between you and the centre. No total qualification time is published for this award either, so there is no official hours figure against which to sanity-check a proposed timetable. Before paying anything, ask for the cohort dates, an expected completion date, and a price that separates tuition from registration and certification.</p>', 5],
                ['How does it compare with the Level 7 IT diploma?', '<p>They answer different questions. The IT award is broad computing — networks, databases, information systems, a delivery project and a subject on technology\'s effect on society — and it publishes a total study time of 1,200 hours. This one is narrow and quantitative: statistics, modelling, forecasting and machine learning, with three named languages. If your work is building and running systems, the IT award fits better. If your work is extracting answers from data, this is the closer match.</p>', 6],
            ],
